WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:10.185
Okay. You've written a little bit about something called the imposter syndrome.

2
00:00:10.185 --> 00:00:15.450
What is the imposter syndrome and how can students overcome it?

3
00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:21.880
Okay, the imposter syndrome is like a tape that plays inside people's heads,

4
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.615
not just students, a lot of people.

5
00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:28.495
Let's do it in a student context though.

6
00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:29.550
The tape is like this,

7
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.595
so the student is sitting in class,

8
00:00:31.595 --> 00:00:34.305
looking around and thinking,

9
00:00:34.305 --> 00:00:36.820
"Boy, these people are really good.

10
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.545
They get this stuff,

11
00:00:38.545 --> 00:00:41.140
they can answer the teacher's questions,

12
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.260
they can do the homework,

13
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.160
they can do fine on the test.

14
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:53.505
But I," the tape inside the head continues, "I'm really not."

15
00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:57.490
Right? I've managed to fool them all over the years,

16
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.120
the tape goes, my friends,

17
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.915
my family, my teachers,

18
00:01:00.915 --> 00:01:04.020
into thinking that I'm this real hotshot who belongs at

19
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.350
this first-class university taking engineering,

20
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.415
taking science, taking economics, whatever I'm taking.

21
00:01:10.415 --> 00:01:12.935
But I know better.

22
00:01:12.935 --> 00:01:17.330
The tape continues, "The very next tough question I get in class,

23
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.735
the very next hard test that I have to take,

24
00:01:20.735 --> 00:01:25.240
that's what's going to finally once and for all reveal me as the fraud,

25
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.395
the phony, the imposter that I know I am."

26
00:01:29.395 --> 00:01:37.310
Then what happens then is usually too horrifying to contemplate and so at that point,

27
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:39.280
the imposter usually stops the tape,

28
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.090
rewinds it, and gets it going again.

29
00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.285
The interesting thing about that phenomenon is how utterly common it is.

30
00:01:48.285 --> 00:01:54.350
But every student playing that tape imagines that he or she is the only one who's doing

31
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.860
it and the idea that a lot of people are

32
00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.700
doing it and that hotshot in the front row with a 4.0 average,

33
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.145
the tape is playing louder than it is in anyone else's head,

34
00:02:05.145 --> 00:02:07.110
comes as a surprise to them.

35
00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.400
The reason that I'm so sure about how common this phenomenon

36
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.915
is is that I've given this talk many times to students.

37
00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:18.830
So, I am in an auditorium full of students that I'm giving this talk to and when I get to

38
00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:24.290
the part about they all think I belong here but I know better,

39
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.120
it's like I plucked a guitar string.

40
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.645
There is a quiver that goes over the entire audience

41
00:02:29.645 --> 00:02:34.860
and eyes dilate and jaws go slack and then they start

42
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.220
checking each other out and there's this tangible relaxation that

43
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:44.110
goes over the entire room as they realize this is just a scam.

44
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:47.635
I'm playing on myself and it is a scam.

45
00:02:47.635 --> 00:02:53.190
If a student managed to do everything needed to get into this college,

46
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.510
to pass all of the entrance requirements,

47
00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:59.810
maybe to get through that difficult first year, then obviously,

48
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.620
they have the ability to do it because they did it.

49
00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.410
So, when I make that speech, as I say,

50
00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:12.400
it relaxes a lot of students and once you're familiar with that phenomenon,

51
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.885
what happens is imposters keep showing up.

52
00:03:15.885 --> 00:03:21.550
In my office, not a week goes by that I don't see two or three imposters

53
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.800
coming into my office and as soon as they start

54
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.700
talking about the problems that they're having,

55
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.170
"Okay, here's another imposter."

56
00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:35.045
I reach into the left middle drawer of my desk in a file folder there.

57
00:03:35.045 --> 00:03:37.900
I pull out a copy of a little column that I

58
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.905
wrote on the imposter phenomenon and I say, "Here.

59
00:03:40.905 --> 00:03:43.650
Check this out. You might find it intriguing."

60
00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:50.965
So, this imposter in my office reads his or her biography and it's high drama.

61
00:03:50.965 --> 00:03:55.500
I get the tears from male and female imposters.

62
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.945
I get practically screaming.

63
00:03:57.945 --> 00:04:04.230
I had one woman student accuse me of stealing her diary.

64
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.610
So, it's a really good thing for teachers to know about and it's a very,

65
00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:14.920
very good thing for students to know about because we're all playing it.

66
00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:23.070
It may surprise you to know that a lot of us have that imposter feeling often

67
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.610
and that's something that each of us has

68
00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:33.100
struggled with in our whole adult lives as well as some of our students.

69
00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.995
The power, I think,

70
00:04:35.995 --> 00:04:37.180
is in naming it,

71
00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.450
oh, it's the imposter.

72
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:43.270
Once you realize that that's what you're doing,

73
00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.970
then it diffuses a lot of the power.

74
00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.395
You can just remind yourself,

75
00:04:48.395 --> 00:04:51.445
"Okay, I know how to do this, I can.

76
00:04:51.445 --> 00:04:53.205
This may be something new,

77
00:04:53.205 --> 00:04:55.390
but I've met challenges before.

78
00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.195
I'll meet this one.

79
00:04:57.195 --> 00:05:01.940
I can do it," and go ahead and push on.

80
00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.760
You'll find that as you get into whatever the task may be,

81
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.330
that you settle down and you realize you really

82
00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.525
can do it and you have resources if you have trouble.

83
00:05:11.525 --> 00:05:15.745
Right. So, now, let's turn to something a little different.

84
00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:19.060
What do you both do to avoid

85
00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.470
procrastination when it comes to something you'd really rather not be doing.

86
00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.300
Do you ever struggle with this?

87
00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.590
It's hard to think that two of these.

88
00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.005
Let me get to you later on that.

89
00:05:30.005 --> 00:05:31.860
Yeah.

90
00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.430
When we figure it out,

91
00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.500
we'll let you know.

92
00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.850
No, we definitely both struggle

93
00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:44.450
with it as I'm sure you knew when you asked that question.

94
00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.250
We found some strategies,

95
00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.845
[inaudible] for each of us that work,

96
00:05:50.845 --> 00:05:54.125
but it is still something we struggle with.

97
00:05:54.125 --> 00:06:01.270
I like to try to schedule myself if I've got a big task,

98
00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:06.390
schedule a small amount of time every day to work on it,

99
00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.090
to do just a little bit.

100
00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:14.030
Not try to do everything all at once but to bite off a small chunk.

101
00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:19.760
I've also found it to be really helpful where I keep a to-do list on

102
00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:27.610
my phone and if I break the task down into smaller pieces,

103
00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:32.210
whats the very next thing that I need to do that's manageable,

104
00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:38.840
then that makes it less overwhelming and I'm able to get going on it.

105
00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.425
Once you get started,

106
00:06:40.425 --> 00:06:41.970
it's easier to keep going.

107
00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.855
Oh, absolutely.

108
00:06:43.855 --> 00:06:48.655
This is one area where Rebecca and I are exactly on the same page.

109
00:06:48.655 --> 00:06:51.905
So, I use the same strategies.

110
00:06:51.905 --> 00:06:56.390
There were two points in the strategy that she mentioned which I'll just reiterate.

111
00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:59.830
One is breaking it up into small pieces.

112
00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:03.430
If I think I've got to write the book or I've got to write

113
00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:08.175
the dissertation or I've got to write the 25-page proposal,

114
00:07:08.175 --> 00:07:13.545
it's Mount Everest and it's very hard to get myself to take that first step.

115
00:07:13.545 --> 00:07:15.160
But if I say,

116
00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:24.145
"I've got to write the first two pages of the introduction to this thing," that I can do.

117
00:07:24.145 --> 00:07:25.815
That's not that formidable.

118
00:07:25.815 --> 00:07:31.685
So, if I just break it up into small pieces and set easy targets for myself,

119
00:07:31.685 --> 00:07:33.510
then I can keep marching.

120
00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.410
Then the other suggestion that she made which I would agree

121
00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:40.530
with is make appointments with yourself.

122
00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:44.405
So, if I work well in the morning,

123
00:07:44.405 --> 00:07:46.280
I'm a morning person,

124
00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.800
I think well, I'm clear headed then,

125
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.665
then 10 O'clock to 10:30 every morning on my calendar,

126
00:07:54.665 --> 00:07:56.460
I make an appointment with myself,

127
00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.670
I'm going to work on that proposal for

128
00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:05.150
that half hour then I'll stop and I'll go back to the rest of my life.

129
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.300
Next day, 10 O'clock,

130
00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.235
assuming I don't have other obligations at 10 O'clock,

131
00:08:10.235 --> 00:08:13.130
I'll do another half hour on it.

132
00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:18.280
It's incredible how stuff gets done when you can do things on a regular basis and

133
00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.950
the ones that you're most reluctant to do that you're

134
00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.950
putting off because I don't want to do that,

135
00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.310
those are the ones that you've got to schedule appointments with yourself.

136
00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:32.440
What a lot of people do is they work on the block of time theory and a time

137
00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:37.455
to work on the proposal now or the term paper now,

138
00:08:37.455 --> 00:08:39.115
but as soon as it's the weekend,

139
00:08:39.115 --> 00:08:41.680
as soon as this fall break or anything like that,

140
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.320
that's when I really get it done, and it doesn't work.

141
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.860
Among other things, as soon as you get to this block of time,

142
00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:51.180
other things rush in to fill it.

143
00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:54.920
Also, it can be so long since the last time you worked on it,

144
00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.660
you forgot what you were doing,

145
00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.335
and by the time you figure it out and get your momentum back up,

146
00:09:00.335 --> 00:09:02.100
the block of time may be over.

147
00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:08.280
But the steady appointment to just do the next chunk for half an hour, it'll get done.

148
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.355
Some people find some form of accountability is helpful.

149
00:09:12.355 --> 00:09:16.840
A charge where they're checking off the stamps or

150
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:22.700
a friend who will check in with them regularly,

151
00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.910
"How's it going on that project?

152
00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:26.425
Are you making progress?"

153
00:09:26.425 --> 00:09:29.640
But those kinds of things can be good strategies to.

154
00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:33.915
Absolutely. Well, I'm a big believer in the half hour chunk.

155
00:09:33.915 --> 00:09:36.830
That time frame seems to work really well for me for

156
00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:40.165
something that I'm having difficulty completing.

157
00:09:40.165 --> 00:09:43.430
I would like to thank you ever so much for

158
00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:48.730
your incredible responses that I think people will find enormously helpful.

159
00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.890
It's a great pleasure.